"""Additional criterion-specific teaching for the Understandable lessons."""

EXTENSION = {
    '3.1.1': ('Inheritance and page templates', 'The language on the root element is inherited by its descendants unless a more specific language is set. In a multi-page application, check the root element generated for each page rather than only the shared template. In a single-page application, changing visible content to another primary language may require updating the document language as part of that transition. A regional tag can add useful detail, but only if it accurately describes the content.', 'A common failure occurs when a translated page changes its words while retaining the original template’s language. Compare the text and root attribute after navigating between translations, including after a reload. Automated checks can detect a missing or invalid tag, but a person must still determine whether the tag describes the actual language.'),
    '3.1.2': ('Scope and inheritance', 'Language attributes are inherited, so place a change on the smallest element that contains the passage in the other language. If a whole quotation is French, mark the quotation rather than every word. If the quotation contains a nested English phrase, that phrase may need its own language declaration so that the inherited French does not continue through it.', 'An interface that uses a flag icon to indicate language can help sighted readers, but an icon does not set pronunciation rules for text. Inspect the actual element containing the words. When testing with speech, verify both the foreign passage and the return to the surrounding language; an available voice and language-switching settings can affect the result.'),
    '3.1.3': ('Choosing a useful definition', 'A definition is most useful when it explains a word in the same sense as the current task. For example, in a course about accounts, a session is the period during which an authenticated interaction is maintained, not a meeting in a classroom. Introduce that meaning where the term first becomes relevant and link it to a glossary if readers may encounter it later without the introduction.', 'Check the definition in the context of a real instruction. Ask a learner to describe what expires when the page says a session has expired. If the learner can read the definition but still cannot understand the action, revise the surrounding explanation as well. Do not hide essential meaning exclusively behind a tooltip that requires a mouse.'),
    '3.1.4': ('First use and repeated entry points', 'A learner may enter documentation through a search result and never see the first occurrence on the home page. Consider how each page can provide an expansion or a reachable glossary, particularly when a shortened term is central to an instruction. The same abbreviation can mean different things in different fields, so choose the expansion that fits the current subject.', 'A visible expansion supports readers even when browser or assistive-technology support for title tooltips varies. If a glossary link is used, give it a meaningful name and make sure it can be followed and returned from with the keyboard. Review labels and compact navigation too: shortening a control name without explaining it can make the action hard to identify.'),
    '3.1.5': ('Designing an alternative', 'Begin by identifying what the reader needs to decide or do. Separate conditions, actions, and consequences into a sequence, then define unavoidable specialized words. For example, explain which document a student must upload, when it is due, and what happens if it is missing. A short summary that omits one of these requirements is not an effective substitute for the complete instruction.', 'Supplemental material may include an accessible illustration or a worked example, but it should correspond to the original information. Invite a reader unfamiliar with the process to perform a representative task and explain the result. A readability score may reveal long sentences; it cannot determine whether a particular instruction is accurate, complete, or understandable.'),
    '3.1.6': ('Meaning, context and pronunciation', 'A pronunciation guide needs to identify the word in its actual sentence. For example, “record” can refer to a stored item or the action of recording, with different stress patterns in English. If the sentence already makes the intended meaning clear, the specific ambiguity addressed by this criterion may not arise; evaluate the passage instead of adding phonetic text to every occurrence.', 'If audio is offered, check that a learner can find and operate it without a mouse. Written phonetic guidance or a clear explanation can help someone who cannot hear the recording. Verify pronunciation with a reliable language specialist and test whether the explanation communicates the intended sense to the intended audience.'),
    '3.2.1': ('Focus is not activation', 'Keyboard focus is a position in the interface, not a decision. Pressing Tab may move through several links before the learner chooses one. If focus itself opens a new page or submits a form, a person cannot inspect the options safely. Use the focus event for feedback such as a visible indicator, and use an explicit activation event for navigation or submission.', 'Test forward and backward keyboard movement through every interactive item. Also check script-driven focus after an error or dialog closes: moving focus to explain an error is different from using focus to trigger a new context. Record the starting control, action, destination, and whether navigation was intentional so the result is reproducible.'),
    '3.2.2': ('Distinguishing input from commitment', 'A learner can change a selection as part of exploring choices. If the application immediately sends them to another page on the first arrow-key change, the learner may never reach the desired option. A separate Apply button makes the commitment explicit, while text before the control can describe an immediate context change when that behavior is intended.', 'Not every live update is a context change. A filter that updates a list in place without moving focus or navigating needs accessibility review, including announcement of important results, but should not automatically be treated as the same case as a redirect. Test with keyboard, pointer, and assistive technology, and observe the actual change rather than judging from the event name alone.'),
    '3.2.3': ('Comparing relative order', 'Relative order means that repeated links retain their sequence when they appear together. A page may add a local link without moving Home, Courses, and Help relative to one another. The criterion does not require every page to contain all the same links, nor does it prevent a learner from choosing a personalized order.', 'To review a site, select several pages within the same set and list their repeated navigation items in the order encountered. Compare both the visual presentation and keyboard sequence, particularly at narrow widths where a menu may collapse. A design that appears consistent but places repeated links in a different focus order can still confuse a keyboard user.'),
    '3.2.4': ('Recognizing the same function', 'A function is what a control or component does, not merely the shape of its icon. If a magnifying-glass button opens search on several pages, give it a consistent identification so a learner can recognize the same action. A familiar icon can supplement a label, but the accessible name must also communicate the function to someone who does not see the icon.', 'Review repeated controls across page templates, including mobile variants. Similar wording can be consistent even if grammar differs in context; conversely, identical-looking icons can be misleading when they perform different actions. Compare the control’s action, visible label, and accessible name together before deciding whether it is identified consistently.'),
    '3.2.5': ('Transitions initiated by the user', 'A change of context can include moving to a different page, opening a new window, or moving focus in a way that substantially changes the user’s interaction. A timed redirect after a lesson may remove the explanation before a reader is ready. Prefer a clearly labeled Next button, or provide a setting that lets the learner prevent an automatic transition.', 'Trace timers, background responses, focus handlers, and changes to form values. Ask whether a transition was initiated by the user or whether there was an accessible mechanism to turn it off. A small in-place update does not necessarily have the same effect as changing the page or focus; assess what the learner actually experiences.'),
    '3.2.6': ('Findable help across pages', 'A learner who encounters an error may remember that Help followed the course navigation. If the same contact method later appears before the logo or only inside an unrelated menu, finding it again takes extra effort. Keep repeated help mechanisms in a consistent relative order within the set of pages where they occur.', 'Compare the placement of the same support link, contact information, self-help option, or automated contact mechanism across representative pages. Responsive layouts can present the same order in different ways; check the actual reading and keyboard order as well. The criterion concerns the order of repeated mechanisms and does not, by itself, require a site to offer a particular type of help.'),
    '3.3.1': ('Identifying the problem in context', 'An error message needs to tell the learner which item is affected and what went wrong in text. “Please enter an email address” identifies a missing value more clearly than turning an input border red. For several failures, a summary can name each item while local messages remain available next to the controls. Keep the text accurate when the server reports an error after submission.', 'Associate local error text with the relevant input and verify that a screen reader can discover it. Test an empty required field, an invalid format, and an error returned by the server. Confirm that the entered values remain available for correction and that the user can reach the affected field from the summary without hunting through the whole form.'),
    '3.3.2': ('Labels and instructions serve different purposes', 'A visible label tells the learner what information a control collects. Instructions explain additional conditions, such as a required format, a range, or whether the field is mandatory. For example, “Date of birth” names the field; “Use day/month/year” explains how to enter the value. Place the instruction where it remains available when the user begins typing.', 'A placeholder is not a dependable replacement for a label because it disappears or becomes harder to see as text is entered. Programmatically associate the label and relevant instructions with the field, and use a fieldset and legend when a group of related choices needs a shared question. Test both visible presentation and the name and description announced for the input.'),
    '3.3.3': ('Suggestions that match the actual rule', 'If a date is rejected because the application expects a particular format, an error such as “Enter a date as DD/MM/YYYY, for example 25/04/2026” helps the user repair it. If the system knows that a number is outside an accepted range, a safe message can explain that range. Retain the value already entered so the learner can correct it instead of starting again.', 'Do not reveal information that would compromise security or the purpose of the content. For example, a login error may need to avoid confirming whether a particular account exists. Check that every suggestion matches the current validation rules and that it is associated with the field. A useful suggestion complements, rather than replaces, identifying the error itself.'),
    '3.3.4': ('Comparing prevention mechanisms', 'For a payment, a review step can show the amount, recipient, and other consequential values before final confirmation. A learner must be able to return and correct a mistake without losing unrelated information. Alternatively, a genuinely reversible operation or checks that identify errors and allow correction can provide one of the criterion’s specified safeguards.', 'Test the safeguard with a deliberately wrong value. A generic “Are you sure?” prompt that does not show what will be submitted may not give the learner a useful opportunity to review. If relying on reversal, verify that it actually restores the prior state; if relying on input checking, verify that a detected error can be corrected before the transaction is committed.'),
    '3.3.5': ('Help tailored to the task', 'Context-sensitive help answers a question the learner is likely to have at a particular point. Near an account-name field, explain the accepted characters and give a valid example. A general help home page may contain the answer, but it forces the learner to search and infer which article applies. Keep concise guidance near the field and link to longer explanations when needed.', 'If help opens in a dialog, verify that it is keyboard operable, its contents are announced, and closing it returns focus to a sensible location. Opening or closing help should not discard entered values. After validation rules change, update examples and help text together; instructions that suggest an invalid value can create the very error they should prevent.'),
    '3.3.6': ('Useful safeguards for routine changes', 'A profile form may not involve payment, but accidentally changing a setting can still be disruptive. A review step allows the learner to inspect values before saving; validation can identify a detectable input error and offer correction; a reliable Undo can reverse the change afterward. The criterion provides these approaches as alternatives, so select a mechanism that makes sense for the submission.', 'Run a realistic scenario rather than checking that an Undo label exists. Change a setting, save it, use Undo, and confirm that the old value is restored. For a review mechanism, edit a value from the review screen and verify that the final saved record contains the correction. Document limitations so a user is not promised a reversal that the system cannot perform.'),
    '3.3.7': ('Reuse without forcing an incorrect value', 'A checkout may collect a delivery address and later ask for billing information. Offer the delivery address as a selectable option or prefill the repeated information, while letting a learner enter a different billing address. Reuse is valuable because it reduces memory and typing demands, but the learner should be able to see what was carried forward and correct it.', 'Check the entire process, including moving backward and forward between steps. Confirm that reused data reaches the final submission and that edits do not overwrite the original address unexpectedly. Re-entry is permitted under the criterion’s specified exceptions, including essential re-entry, security, or information that is no longer valid; record the actual reason rather than assuming repetition is always required.'),
    '3.3.8': ('Authentication is a sequence', 'A password field is only one part of authentication. A learner may also face a one-time code, an account-recovery challenge, or a second factor. For each step, identify whether the person must remember, manipulate, or transcribe information, and whether a supported alternative or assistance lets them complete that step. Allowing paste and password-manager fill can reduce the burden of a password test.', 'Test the real flow with the intended browser and devices. A visible paste command is insufficient if the field or application discards pasted text. The minimum criterion includes specific exceptions for object recognition and identification of personal content; those exceptions do not establish that a particular challenge is usable by everyone. Consider recovery and second-factor access rather than evaluating only the first screen.'),
    '3.3.9': ('Enhanced authentication across all paths', 'The enhanced criterion retains the need for an alternative or assisting mechanism where an authentication step uses a cognitive function test, but it does not include the minimum criterion’s object-recognition and personal-content exceptions. A challenge that asks the learner to identify a familiar photograph therefore needs the enhanced criterion’s alternative or assistance if it involves such a test.', 'Map enrollment, ordinary sign-in, second factor, and recovery as separate paths. A device-based option may reduce memory demands, but verify what happens when the device is unavailable and whether the remaining required steps create a barrier. Test password-manager and paste support where relevant. Do not claim that a single button or a particular technology proves the entire flow meets the criterion.'),
}
